From: Kin Watch <watch@kinwatch.dev>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Ignore EAGAIN on udev monitor receive instead of tearing it
 down

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [aurora-specific workaround]

umudev_io_input_cb() treated any failed udev_monitor_receive_device()
call as fatal: it stops watching the fd, which umudev_io_error_cb()
then reacts to by destroying and recreating the entire udev monitor.
That teardown cancels any pending delayed cable-state transfer (see
umudev_cable_state_start_timer()), and on this device it can land the
gadget in charging_only before the corrected cable state is ever
applied -- observed via lsusb as idProduct 0x0afe with no adb.

Confirmed via targeted diagnostic logging (added and removed during
investigation) that the failure is always errno=EAGAIN: the G_IO_IN
wakeup fired but no message was available by the time the read
happened. This is a well known benign race in udev/GLib integrations,
not a sign the monitor is broken -- the fix is to skip the wakeup and
keep watching, not tear anything down. Only a genuine error (anything
other than EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK) should still trigger the existing
teardown-and-restart path.
---
 src/usb_moded-udev.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/src/usb_moded-udev.c
+++ b/src/usb_moded-udev.c
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@
 #include "usb_moded-log.h"
 
 #include <libudev.h>
+#include <errno.h>
 
 /* ========================================================================= *
  * Constants
@@ -1011,8 +1012,13 @@
         /* This normally blocks but G_IO_IN indicates that we can read */
         struct udev_device *dev = udev_monitor_receive_device(umudev_monitor);
         if( !dev ) {
-            /* if we get something else something bad happened stop watching to avoid busylooping */
-            continue_watching = FALSE;
+            /* EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK: the G_IO_IN wakeup fired but no message
+             * was available by the time we read -- a known benign race in
+             * udev/GLib integrations, not a sign the monitor is broken.
+             * Anything else is a real error: stop watching to avoid
+             * busylooping. */
+            if( errno != EAGAIN && errno != EWOULDBLOCK )
+                continue_watching = FALSE;
         }
         else {
             const char *syspath   = udev_device_get_syspath(dev);
